WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.350
Pat Garrett once admitted that the thing he
feared most was having his life defined by

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:10.080
just one moment - the killing of William H.
Bonney, better known as Billy the Kid. He

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.840
said he dreaded meeting new people because
they always said something along the lines

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.380
of “Hey! You’re Pat Garrett, the guy who
shot Billy the Kid!”

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:22.119
Unfortunately for the lawman, his worst fear
came to pass. Not only in his lifetime, but

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:27.880
also in modern times, his legacy is inexorably
tied to the killing of Bonney, which I guess

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:33.300
is just what happens when you gun down one
of the biggest icons of the Wild West.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:38.360
There was another problem. As the outlaw’s
reputation grew and he was turned into a legend

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:44.140
of the Old West, sympathy for him increased
and Garrett, slowly but surely, was becoming

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:49.460
the villain of this tale. Sure, Billy the
Kid was a ruthless murderer who killed many

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.870
men in cold blood, but people could not help
but feel like Garrett acted somewhat cowardly

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:58.870
by gunning him down in the dark. That was
not how the heroes of the Wild West acted

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:03.070
in their imaginations. If the two had met
in a duel, then the story would probably have

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:07.609
been remembered vastly different. Bonus “cool
points” for Garrett if he would have said

00:01:07.609 --> 00:01:11.520
a snappy one-liner before shooting and lit
a cigar afterwards.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.249
As it stands, though, Pat Garrett sits in
a grey area of western lore where some people

00:01:17.249 --> 00:01:23.479
admire him as a tough lawman in a lawless
land and others brand him a coward. But, as

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:27.970
we are about to see, his life continued with
other thrills and adventures after the death

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:33.319
of Bonney, culminating with Garrett’s own
mysterious murder that still puzzles people

00:01:33.319 --> 00:01:46.829
to this day.
Early Years

00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:52.289
He was born Patrick Floyd Jarvis Garrett on
June 5, 1850, in Chambers County, Alabama,

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:57.240
to John Lumpkin Garrett and Elizabeth Jarvis.
At a young age, he relocated with his family

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.380
to Louisiana after his father bought a cotton
plantation in Claiborne Parish.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:07.189
At first, things went pretty well for the
Garrett family as the plantation flourished,

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:11.690
but things took a drastic turn for the worse
when the Civil War broke out. It completely

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:16.580
ruined the family’s finances and then both
parents died less than a year apart in 1867

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:22.500
and 1868, respectively, leaving behind a plantation
full of debt. Pat’s younger siblings were

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.860
taken in by various relatives. However, as
Garrett turned 18 years old, he saw no reason

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:31.580
to stick around anymore and, in 1869, rode
alone into the sunset.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:32.580
Image: Bison skull pyramid
https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bison_skull_pile_edit.jpg

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:36.300
Very few details are known about the next
years of Garrett’s life. He settled in Texas

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.361
for a while, near the Dallas area, where he
worked as a farmhand. A few years later, he

00:02:40.361 --> 00:02:45.180
became a buffalo hunter on the Staked Plains
and started a business selling their hides.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.600
However, this was not a sustainable career.
People like Garrett almost hunted the American

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:55.160
buffalo (which is actually a bison if we are
being technical) into extinction. Tens of

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:59.560
millions of specimens were killed during the
19th century, at one point reducing their

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.890
numbers to only a few hundred in the entire
country. Once the supply started running out,

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:09.140
Garrett left Texas and drifted towards Fort
Sumner in New Mexico.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:13.630
Before we get there, however, there is still
another important event from Pat Garrett’s

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:19.120
life to discuss - the first time he killed
a man. It happened around 1876 and the victim

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.850
was a friend of Garrett’s and fellow buffalo
hunter named Joe Briscoe. The two of them

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:28.330
got into an argument, who knows over what,
and it escalated to the point of violence.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:33.250
An enraged Briscoe charged at Garrett with
an axe, prompting the latter to grab a pistol

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:37.940
and shoot his attacker at point-blank range.
Allegedly, as he lay there dying, Briscoe

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:43.430
was the one who asked forgiveness of his shooter,
reducing Garrett to tears. Racked with guilt,

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:47.010
the killer tried to turn himself in at Fort
Griffin, but the authorities there had no

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.230
interest in prosecuting him so Garrett just
went on with his life.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:51.230
Image: Peter Maxwell ranch
https://www.legendsofamerica.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/06/Pete-Maxwells-house-1882.jpg

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:55.810
When he arrived in New Mexico, Garrett found
work as a cowboy again at Pedro “Pete”

00:03:55.810 --> 00:04:00.100
Maxwell’s ranch. This is the same house
where he would kill Billy the Kid a few years

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:04.650
later. During this time, he also married a
woman named Juanita Gutierrez who would die

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:08.600
less than a year into their marriage during
childbirth. Garrett would not stay single

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:13.850
for long, though, as in 1880 he remarried
to Apolinaria Gutierrez, the 17-year-old younger

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:18.090
sister of his first wife. They would go on
to have eight children together.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:25.900
The Lincoln County War
That same year, Garrett was elected sheriff

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:30.620
of Lincoln County, New Mexico Territory, and
immediately set his sights on Billy the Kid

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:34.590
and the other gunslingers he rode with.
By this point, Billy the Kid had already earned

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:38.680
his fame thanks to his involvement in the
Lincoln County War which you can learn about

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.833
in-depth in our Biographics video on “Billy
the Kid”.

00:04:40.833 --> 00:04:42.150
Link suggestion: Billy the Kid
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gt303JRUCsE

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:47.210
Basically, the Lincoln County War was a violent
feud that started in 1878 between two factions

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:52.540
that both wanted a monopoly over the dry goods
business. On one side there was the older,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:58.180
established group led by James Dolan and Lawrence
Murphy who already operated the largest general

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:03.100
goods store in Lincoln County known simply
as “The House”. On the other was the new

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:09.510
partnership between businessmen John Tunstall
and Alexander McSween who opened a rival store.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:14.320
To add fuel to the fire, both groups also
had some serious backup in the form of gangs

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.531
willing to protect their side’s interests
with violent diligence. Dolan’s faction

00:05:19.531 --> 00:05:24.600
had the Jesse Evans Gang, better known as
the Boys, and also had the county sheriff

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:29.950
William J. Brady in his pocket. The newcomers
had the Regulators, a group of loosely-affiliated

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:34.889
cowboys and ranch hands who had a history
with one another. They included Billy the

00:05:34.889 --> 00:05:40.490
Kid and other desperados such as Dick Brewer,
Doc Scurlock, the Coe cousins, Tom O’Folliard,

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:43.610
and Charlie Bowdre.
The Lincoln County War kicked off properly

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:48.840
on February 18, 1878, when the Boys killed
John Tunstall. That’s when the Regulators

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.810
formed into a more cohesive gang and, at the
start at least, even had the law on their

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:58.870
side as they received warrants from Justice
of the Peace John Wilson to bring Tunstall’s

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:02.520
killers to justice.
This feud was marked by multiple shootouts

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.980
and revenge killings. Billy the Kid and a
few other Regulators killed Sheriff Brady.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:11.900
Although dozens of other people died, this
was the murder that would send Bonney on the

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:17.419
run with Pat Garrett on his trail. The war
culminated with a five-day shootout known

00:06:17.419 --> 00:06:22.960
as the Battle of Lincoln where Alexander McSween
was also killed. With both businessmen dead,

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.590
the Regulators had nobody to work for anymore
so they scattered.

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:34.100
The First Pursuit
By the time Garrett started chasing Billy

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:39.011
the Kid, the latter was already a notorious
gunman known throughout the country. He and

00:06:39.011 --> 00:06:43.420
a few other Regulators like Bowdre and O’Folliard
decided to stick together after the Lincoln

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:49.020
County War and engage in additional acts of
mayhem, violence, and murder. They also joined

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.780
up with “Dirty” Dave Rudabaugh, another
infamous outlaw of the Old West.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:55.010
Image: Dave Rudabaugh
Billy the Kid’s gang first crossed guns

00:06:55.010 --> 00:07:00.699
with Garrett in late 1880. On December 19,
the gang rode into Fort Sumner, New Mexico,

00:07:00.699 --> 00:07:05.699
where Garrett was already waiting for them,
assisted by another lawman named Lon Chambers.

00:07:05.699 --> 00:07:10.660
He confronted Tom O’Folliard and another
former Regulator named Tom Pickett. When O’Folliard

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:15.260
went for his gun, Garrett shot him in the
chest. He died less than an hour later, aged

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:18.229
22, but the other outlaws managed to make
a run for it.

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:19.229
Image: Tom O’Folliard
The lawman stayed in hot pursuit. Just a few

00:07:19.229 --> 00:07:23.650
days later, on December 23, he tracked the
whole gang to an abandoned farmhouse near

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:28.270
Stinking Springs, New Mexico, and, this time,
he had a large posse with him. It seemed like

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:33.639
Garrett had the situation completely in control,
but the gang still preferred a shootout over

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:37.370
surrender. Former Regulator Charlie Bowdre
was killed during this encounter. The rest

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:41.440
eventually came to their senses and gave up
after a two-day siege.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:47.210
Image: Charlie Bowdre
The Kid Escapes and Second Pursuit

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:51.040
If this would have been the end of the road
for Billy the Kid, Garrett might have been

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.980
unanimously remembered as a hero. He was brave
and diligent in his pursuit of the outlaws,

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:00.199
only killed when absolutely necessary and
managed to bring all the other criminals to

00:08:00.199 --> 00:08:05.080
justice. Unsurprisingly, Bonney was convicted
for the murder of Sheriff Brady and sentenced

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:10.040
to hang until he was “dead, dead, dead”,
which is what the judge told him, according

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:13.610
to legend.
However, on April 28, while awaiting execution,

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:18.130
Bonney managed to outmaneuver his guards.
When only one deputy named James Bell was

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:22.330
watching him, he asked to be taken to the
outhouse where he attacked his sentry. The

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:26.880
Kid shot and killed Bell with his own gun.
Afterwards, he armed himself with a shotgun,

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.430
waited for Bob Ollinger, the other guard,
to return and killed him, too. Bonney then

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.570
jumped on a horse and rode out of town.
Image suggestion: Ollinger plaque

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.540
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Bob_Ollinger_death_marker.JPG
Garrett wasn’t in town when Billy the Kid

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:38.610
escaped, but he soon returned and started
chasing him again.

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:42.200
A few months later, the lawman heard that
his fugitive had returned to Fort Sumner.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:46.930
He went to visit Pete Maxwell’s ranch because
he suspected that Bonney might be hiding there.

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:52.480
The exact sequence of events is up for some
debate but, according to the most common version,

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.850
on the night of July 14, Garrett was sitting
in the dark in Maxwell’s bedroom. Bonney

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:02.940
entered the room and asked who was there and,
upon recognizing his voice, the sheriff shot

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:09.620
him twice without saying anything. Billy the
Kid was dead before he hit the ground.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:16.420
Aftermath
Word of the outlaw’s demise traveled fast.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:20.730
As the story of Billy the Kid was plastered
on every newspaper in the country, Bonney

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:25.690
was slowly being turned into a folk hero rather
than a lifelong criminal who was on the run

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:30.360
after mercilessly killing two deputies. Moreover,
Garrett was the one that the media chose to

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:35.670
portray as the cold-blooded assassin.
He couldn’t stand this so, in 1882, he wrote

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:39.970
his own account with the help of a ghostwriter
to set the record straight. It was titled

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:44.190
“The Authentic Life of Billy, the Kid”
and it became, for a while, the definitive

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:48.910
version of the events that went down at Peter
Maxwell’s ranch. However, it was later found

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:53.650
to contain many inaccuracies and historians
dismissed it as an embellished narrative with

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:57.500
the occasional truth thrown in.
While the newspapers went way overboard with

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:01.600
their sympathetic portrayal of Billy the Kid
because it made a better story, Garrett’s

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:06.940
account veered too much in the opposite direction
as its main goal was to clear his own name

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.730
rather than give a factual description of
the events.

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:14.080
Most notably, Garrett’s version changes
the part where he gunned down Bonney in the

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:19.220
dark without giving him a chance to surrender.
In a “Han shot first”-type scenario, the

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:24.050
sheriff alleged that the Kid raised his cocked
pistol a foot away from Garrett, thus leaving

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:33.140
him with no choice but to pull the trigger.
Businessman Garrett

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:37.360
Garrett did not seek re-election as sheriff
and, instead, had a brief stint with the Texas

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:41.250
Rangers. After that was over, he left law
enforcement for about a decade, time during

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:45.850
which he tried to enter the irrigation industry.
Garrett lived on a large ranch near Roswell,

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:50.420
New Mexico. He soon realized that in many
arid, undeveloped regions of the Southwest,

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:54.670
irrigation was a necessity for any kind of
successful agriculture. In his area, that

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:58.990
irrigation would have come courtesy of the
Pecos River which Garrett thought could be

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:04.089
dammed and used to construct a large-scale
canal. One anecdote that definitely isn’t

00:11:04.089 --> 00:11:07.839
true even stated that he originally got the
idea from Billy the Kid who told him that

00:11:07.839 --> 00:11:12.300
he could grow crops and make lots of money
instead of chasing guys like him.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:16.810
During the mid 1880s, Garrett met businessman
Charles Eddy who was in the process of buying

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:21.730
as much land as possible with his own plans
to invest into an irrigation system. Eventually,

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:26.800
the two decided to combine their ventures
for one large reclamation project. They were

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:32.350
also joined by a third entrepreneur called
Charles Greene and, in 1888, the “Pecos

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:36.470
Irrigation and Investment Company” was born.
As the company grew and more businessmen joined

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:40.460
the project, Garrett was slowly relegated
to an increasingly smaller role. Although

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:44.550
this could have been done because others were
simply more experienced than him, one Garrett

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:49.040
biographer asserts that it was a deliberate
effort to push him out of the company.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:53.000
The lawman wasn’t quite ready to leave the
business completely so, instead, he purchased

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:57.330
one-third interest in the “Texas Irrigation
Ditch Company” and formed another business

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:02.050
called the “Holloman and Garrett Ditch Company”
with entrepreneur William Holloman. Both companies

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:08.570
failed and, eventually, Garrett abandoned
this foray completely and relocated to Uvalde,

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:13.279
Texas.
The Fountain Mystery

00:12:13.279 --> 00:12:18.220
After almost a decade and a half of relative
obscurity, Pat Garrett was, once again, in

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:23.300
the headlines in 1896 due to his involvement
in the disappearance case of Colonel Albert

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:26.520
Jennings Fountain and his eight-year-old son,
Henry.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:28.980
Image: Albert Fountain
Fountain was an attorney with the Texas Senate

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:33.080
who made a lot of dangerous enemies when he
went after powerful men involved in cattle

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:37.560
rustling in the New Mexico territory. In late
January, 1896, he and his son were traveling

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:41.810
by wagon back home to Mesilla, New Mexico.
They disappeared and were presumed murdered

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:44.459
on February 1 near White Sands in Doña Ana
County.

00:12:44.459 --> 00:12:49.180
Suspicion fell on two wealthy ranchers and
rivals of Fountain - Oliver Lee and Albert

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:53.670
Fall. The scandal was front-page news across
the country. Governor William Thornton wanted

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:58.590
the matter resolved so he coaxed Pat Garrett
out of retirement by making him the Sheriff

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:01.760
of Doña Ana County.
Garrett located the spot where the presumed

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:05.240
murders took place. The ground was soaked
in blood and there was a smaller pool of blood

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:10.610
a few feet away. Searching the area, the sheriff
and his posse also found empty cartridge casings,

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:15.399
Fountain’s tie, some of his papers, and
his buckboard wagon stained with blood. It

00:13:15.399 --> 00:13:19.740
was pretty clear that something very violent
took place there, but investigators were never

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:24.390
able to find the bodies. To make matters more
difficult, tracks that led away from the area

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:29.770
had been “accidentally” erased by a passing
herd of cattle which, by complete coincidence,

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:33.170
belonged to Oliver Lee.
Garrett thought Lee was the culprit, alongside

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:37.870
two of the rancher’s employees, Jim Gililland
and William McNew. The sheriff obtained arrest

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:41.800
warrants for all three. He placed McNew in
custody, but the other two men made a run

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:44.209
for it.
Garrett pursued the murder suspects and, on

00:13:44.209 --> 00:13:50.110
July 12, 1898, he caught up to them at the
Wildy Well Ranch near the Jarilla Mountains.

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:55.640
A shootout ensued, but it did not go in Garrett’s
favor. One of his deputies, Kent Kearney,

00:13:55.640 --> 00:14:00.670
was hit twice. As his situation was deteriorating,
the sheriff was forced to back down and call

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:07.710
a truce so that he could get medical attention
for his deputy. Kearney died on the way to

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:11.260
Las Cruces.
The Trial

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:15.690
Lee and Gililland stayed on the run for another
eight months before finally surrendering.

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:20.950
At trial, they were represented by Albert
Fall, the other landowner who hated Fountain

00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:25.490
and, according to some, might have even orchestrated
the whole assassination plot. Now, Fall was

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:29.680
not just a wealthy rancher. He was also a
good lawyer and an influential politician.

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:34.660
In fact, he would later serve as Secretary
of the Interior under President Warren G.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:38.420
Harding. Thanks to his involvement in the
Teapot Dome Scandal, Fall has the dubious

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:42.690
distinction of being the first U.S. cabinet
member in history to be convicted and serve

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.900
prison time for a felony.
Image: Albert B. Fall

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:48.720
During the trial, Garrett’s reputation worked
against him. Lee argued that the shootout

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:53.370
at Wildy Well was in self-defense because
Pat Garrett had no intention of taking him

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:57.120
and Gililland alive. If they surrendered,
the sheriff would have gunned them down in

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:01.940
cold blood. This story alongside Fall’s
adept defense and the lack of bodies ensured

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:06.860
that the suspects walked away free, not even
being charged in Deputy Kearney’s death.

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:10.680
Not only that, but Oliver Lee went on to have
a successful political career and today there

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:13.981
is a state park named after him in Otero County.
Nobody else was ever charged in connection

00:15:13.981 --> 00:15:17.350
with the murders and the disappearance of
Albert and Henry Fountain remains one of New

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:26.040
Mexico’s biggest unsolved mysteries.
Presidential Appointee

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:30.480
The last highlight of Garrett’s law enforcement
career happened in 1899 when he was pursuing

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:35.899
a fugitive wanted for murder named Norman
Newman. The chase ended in a gunfight where

00:15:35.899 --> 00:15:40.760
the sheriff shot the criminal and, thus, Norman
Newman became, to our knowledge, the last

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:44.360
man killed by Pat Garrett.
Garrett had a stroke of luck in 1901 when

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:49.019
Teddy Roosevelt became President of the United
States. Roosevelt had a deep fondness for

00:15:49.019 --> 00:15:54.470
famed frontier lawmen who he said “correspond
to those Vikings, like Hastings and Rollo,

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:58.910
who finally served the cause of civilization”.
He gave jobs to several of them over the course

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:04.530
of his two terms as president including Seth
Bullock of Deadwood fame and Bat Masterson.

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:09.360
Consequently, in 1901, Pat Garrett became
collector of customs in El Paso.

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:13.180
Garrett’s appointment was not well-received.
Despite numerous complaints, Roosevelt seemed

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:17.710
determined to stick by his man. Allegedly,
the last straw came when Garrett attended

00:16:17.710 --> 00:16:22.220
a presidential party and brought along a guest
named Tom Powers. He introduced him to the

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:26.589
president as a successful cattleman and the
two took several photos together, only for

00:16:26.589 --> 00:16:30.990
Roosevelt to find out later that Powers was,
actually, the owner of a notorious saloon

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:35.720
in El Paso. After that, the president refused
to renew Garrett’s appointment in 1905.

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:41.080
The Strange Demise of Pat Garrett
Image: Jesse Wayne Brazel

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:47.079
Most of the popular icons of the Wild West
met violent ends and Pat Garrett was no exception.

00:16:47.079 --> 00:16:53.360
On February 29, 1908, he was shot on the way
to Las Cruces, New Mexico, and died aged 57.

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:57.871
Ostensibly, his killer was a man named Jesse
Wayne Brazel who acted in self-defense. He

00:16:57.871 --> 00:17:02.680
turned himself in and confessed. At his trial,
he was defended by none other than Albert

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:07.459
Fall and he was acquitted. It would appear
to be case closed, except that none of Garrett’s

00:17:07.459 --> 00:17:11.880
relatives and, indeed, most historians believe
that Brazel was actually the one who pulled

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:14.480
the trigger.
There are a lot of conspiracy theories floating

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:19.339
around regarding Garrett’s murder. Here
are the facts, as best we know them, and you

00:17:19.339 --> 00:17:23.809
can form your own opinion. Garrett was in
a dispute with Brazel over some land that

00:17:23.809 --> 00:17:27.270
the former had leased to the latter for five
years. Garrett wanted to break the lease when

00:17:27.270 --> 00:17:31.120
he discovered that Brazel was going to use
his property for goat grazing. Also involved

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:35.650
was a man named Print Rhode, a friend of Brazel’s
and an enemy of Garrett who had run-ins with

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:40.490
him while still a sheriff. We should also
mention W. W. Cox, a wealthy rancher from

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:44.400
the area who also hated Garrett and possibly
wanted his land.

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:47.910
Eventually, the two sides reached an agreement
where Brazel would terminate his lease if

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:53.521
he had a buyer for his goatherd. That is where
another man named Carl Adamson came in. The

00:17:53.521 --> 00:17:58.970
exact details of the agreement are a bit murky,
but they are besides the point. What mattered

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:03.280
was that on the morning of February 29, Pat
Garrett, Adamson, and Brazel were traveling

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:08.530
together to Las Cruces. That is when Brazel
shot Garrett twice and then turned himself

00:18:08.530 --> 00:18:12.950
in to the Doña Ana County sheriff’s office,
exclaiming “Lock me up! I’ve just killed

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:16.210
Pat Garrett!”
Adamson acted as witness and corroborated

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:21.110
Brazel’s story that an argument caused Garrett
to reach for his shotgun but Brazel drew quicker.

00:18:21.110 --> 00:18:23.890
And, who knows, maybe this was, indeed, what
happened.

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:28.780
There were a few things suspicious about Garrett’s
death such as the fact that, allegedly, the

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:33.350
former sheriff was shot in the back of the
head and this was never brought up at trial.

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:37.760
And speaking of the trial, how come, of all
people, Albert Fall was the one who defended

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:42.080
Brazel? He had close ties to the judge and
the prosecutor in the case and also rancher

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:45.780
W. W. Cox who some see as the mastermind of
this whole plot.

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:50.360
Garrett’s son believed Adamson was the killer
and some agree. Others think it was Print

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:54.580
Rhode who caught up to the trio and took his
revenge. And there’s another major suspect

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:58.870
we haven’t even mentioned yet - an outlaw
named Jim Miller, better known as “Killin’

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:03.901
Jim” or “Killer Miller” who sometimes
acted as gun-for-hire and just happened to

00:19:03.901 --> 00:19:08.660
be in the area. One popular theory says that
Miller was paid by Cox to do the deed.

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:12.870
We probably won’t agree on what happened
since none of us can know for certain. What

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:17.010
we can agree on is that getting shot in the
back of the head and being dumped on the side

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:22.390
of the road was a disgraceful, undeserving
end for a man once called by “Billy the

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:48.350
Kid” historian Walter Noble Burns as the
“last great sheriff of the old frontier”.

